    bio: "Bedřich Hrozný was a Czech philologist and archaeologist whose 1915 decipherment of the Hittite language ranks among the most consequential intellectual achievements in the history of ancient studies. Born in Lysá nad Labem in Bohemia, Hrozný showed an extraordinary aptitude for languages from childhood, eventually mastering over a dozen ancient and modern tongues. He studied Oriental languages at the University of Vienna and in Berlin, and by his early thirties had established himself as a leading expert in cuneiform script and the languages of ancient Mesopotamia.\n\nHis breakthrough came when he turned his attention to a cache of clay tablets written in an unknown cuneiform script that had been excavated at Boğazköy in central Anatolia — the ruins of the Hittite capital Hattusa — and sent to a Berlin museum. Scholars had been puzzling over these tablets for years without progress. Hrozný noticed that certain words appeared in contexts that suggested food and eating, and by comparing them with known Semitic and Indo-European roots, he identified a phrase that seemed to mean 'now you will eat bread.' From this foothold, he worked outward with relentless methodical precision, demonstrating that Hittite was not a Semitic language as most had assumed, but an Indo-European one — a discovery he announced in a landmark lecture in November 1915, in the middle of the First World War, to an audience of stunned colleagues.\n\nThe implications were enormous. If Hittite was Indo-European, the Hittites were among the earliest attested speakers of the language family that includes almost all of Europe\'s modern tongues, as well as Persian, Sanskrit, and their descendants. The entire prehistory of language, migration, and civilisation in Eurasia needed to be reconsidered. Hrozný spent subsequent decades expanding the decipherment, publishing comprehensive grammars and dictionaries of Hittite, and conducting his own excavations in Anatolia and the Near East. He also made important contributions to the study of other ancient Anatolian languages, including Luwian and Palaic.\n\nHrozný lived to see Hittitology develop into a fully fledged academic discipline, though the field he founded quickly surpassed some of his own later, more speculative contributions — he became increasingly bold in attempting to connect ancient scripts and languages in ways his successors judged overstated. He died in Prague in 1952, having spent his final years under the constraints of Communist Czechoslovakia, somewhat isolated from the international scholarly community he had once electrified. His core achievement, however, was unassailable: he gave voice to a civilisation that history had swallowed whole, and in doing so, rewrote the deep origins of the world we live in."
